WebArchiver - archive whole websites with wget
=============================================

WebArchiver copies websites into a directory you can browse offline. It runs
wget for the downloading and does the rest itself:

- several sites at the same time, with a live progress table
- every file stored once: copies, symlinks and aliases on a site are
  detected (big files before they are downloaded, the rest by content)
- links rewritten to relative paths, so a site works from any directory
- safe to stop at any time (Ctrl+C, network loss, crash) and to resume
- updates download only what changed and keep the old versions; any old
  version can be rebuilt
- an archive page (archive.html) and a history page, plain XHTML 1.0
  without scripts
- a request pace that slows down by itself when a site asks for it


Build and install
-----------------

In the dotfiles, install.sh builds it and installs it into ~/.system/bin
(no root needed):

    ~/dotfiles/install.sh webarchiver

By hand:

    make
    make PREFIX=$HOME/.system install

The result is one statically linked program. It needs wget at run time.
Defaults are in config.h (made from config.def.h on the first build);
edit config.h and run the install again to change them.


Use
---

    webarchiver LINKS_FILE [ARCHIVE_DIR]

        LINKS_FILE has one link per line; empty lines and text after # are
        ignored. Links without http:// or https:// are tried over https
        first, then over plain http. Each domain gets its own directory:
            webarchiver sites.txt          ->  ./example.org/
            webarchiver sites.txt archive  ->  ./archive/example.org/
        Run the same command again at any time: unfinished sites are
        resumed, finished ones are updated.

    webarchiver ARCHIVE_DIR

        Update the whole archive: every link ever archived into it
        (remembered in ARCHIVE_DIR/.archive-sites.txt).

    webarchiver --versions [DOMAIN] [ARCHIVE_DIR]

        List the saved versions; with DOMAIN also the changed files.

    webarchiver --restore DOMAIN VERSION [ARCHIVE_DIR]

        Rebuild an old version as ARCHIVE_DIR/DOMAIN@VERSION/.
        VERSION is a number from --versions (1 = oldest), an id, or
        "latest".

    webarchiver --serve [ARCHIVE_DIR] [PORT]

        Show the archive page at http://127.0.0.1:8800/ with a form to add
        websites and a button to update everything. It listens on this
        computer only.


Progress table
--------------

    downloading / resuming / updating   wget fetches pages and images
    big files                           videos, PDFs, archives: checked
                                        for duplicates, then downloaded
    linking                             duplicates, links, version
    ready / updated                     done (updated = it existed before)
    partial                             stopped early; run again to finish
    failed                              see .archive-logs/<site>.linking.log


Settings (environment variables, defaults in config.h)
------------------------------------------------------

    JOBS=10            sites downloaded at the same time
    WAIT=1             a fixed pause between requests to one site; without
                       it the pace is automatic (WAIT_STEPS)
    WAIT_STEPS="0.2 1 3"
    REFRESH=2          seconds between progress updates
    BIG_EXT=mp4,...    extensions treated as big files
    UA=...             browser identity sent to sites
    IPV4=1             use IPv4 only


Files in an archive directory
-----------------------------

    <domain>/              the current version of each site
    <domain>@<version>/    rebuilt old versions (delete them any time)
    archive.html           the archive page
    history.html           every version of every site
    .archive-sites.txt     the links of the archive
    .archive-history/      old copies of changed files, stored once, and
                           the file list of each version
    .archive-state/        flat text files: url maps, hashes, state
    .archive-logs/         the newest wget log and linking log per site

All data files are plain text, one "key<TAB>value" record per line.


Privacy
-------

WebArchiver sends nothing anywhere except the requests wget makes to the
sites you list. Sites see a common browser identity (UA in config.h)
instead of a program name. Times in the pages and version names are UTC,
so they do not reveal a time zone. The pages contain no absolute paths,
so they do not reveal your user name if you share them.

Everything it keeps stays in the archive directory: wget's list of sites
that asked for https (HSTS) is in .archive-state/wget-hsts instead of
~/.wget-hsts, and only the newest run's wget log is kept for each site.
Deleting the archive directory deletes every trace.

Plain http requests show which pages you fetch to anyone on the network;
that is why https is tried first. To hide your address from the sites,
run it through Tor, for example "torsocks webarchiver ARCHIVE_DIR".
